 ],
 "literal": "Oh friend, may you live! In our town / those who unite the separated, did they exist? / Those with staves in hand, with white-haired heads covered in rags, / with those people who say 'Good, good!' / today saying 'It is very great!' in the assembly of that place.",
 "translation": "Live long, my friend! Did there ever exist in our town those who could reunite separated lovers? In the assembly of that place, the elders—holding staves in their hands, their white-haired heads wrapped in rags—who usually say, 'Good, good!' are today saying, 'This is a very great thing!'",
 "modern": "தோழியே, நீ வாழ்க! நம் ஊரில் பிரிந்தவர்களைச் சேர்த்து வைப்பவர்கள் இருந்தார்களா? கையில் தடியை ஏந்தி, நரைத்த தலையில் தலைப்பாகை அணிந்த பெரியவர்கள், எப்போதும் 'நல்லது, நல்லது' என்று சொல்பவர்கள், இன்று அந்த ஊர் அவையிலே 'இது மிகவும் பெரிய காரியம்' என்று கூறுகிறார்களே!",
 "explanation": "The confidante is speaking to the heroine after the hero's arrival or the formal proposal of marriage. She wonders aloud if there were ever people in their town capable of bringing separated lovers back together. She points to the assembly of wise elders, who hold walking sticks and wear white head-cloths over their grey hair. These elders, who normally nod in approval saying \"Good, good,\" are today astonished and declaring this union to be \"a very great thing!\" This public validation by the town's assembly brings immense relief and joy, transitioning their secret love into a socially accepted marriage.",
